# {{.ProviderShortName}} Provider

{{ .Description | trimspace }}

## Advanced: Custom Headers

Most users do not need `custom_headers`. This option is intended for restricted self-hosted Logfire deployments or environments where requests must pass through a proxy, gateway, or edge authentication layer before reaching Logfire.

For example:

```terraform
provider "logfire" {
  api_key  = var.logfire_api_key
  base_url = var.logfire_base_url

  custom_headers = {
    "CF-Access-Client-Id"     = var.cloudflare_access_client_id
    "CF-Access-Client-Secret" = var.cloudflare_access_client_secret
  }
}
```

Header values should be supplied from Terraform variables or another secret source.

The Logfire `api_key` remains the provider's API authentication mechanism. `custom_headers` only configures extra transport headers.

The provider rejects these header names case-insensitively because they are managed by the provider or API client:

- `Authorization`
- `Content-Type`
- `Accept`
- `User-Agent`
- `X-Terraform-Provider-Version`

## Self-hosted compatibility

Self-hosted Logfire instances expose APIs as they are released, so a feature the provider calls may not exist on an older instance. The minimum Logfire release for each feature is listed below; when a call hits a missing API, the provider says which release it requires.

| Feature | Minimum Logfire release | Minimum Helm chart |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Projects, alerts, dashboards, channels, write/read tokens | All supported releases | - |
| `logfire_api_key`, `logfire_gateway_api_key` | v2026-06-09.02 | - |
| `logfire_organization` create and list, organization import | v2026-06-03.01 | - |
| `logfire_organization` read, update, delete (including setting `billing_email` at creation) | v2026-06-25.01 | - |
| Provider `organization`, and `organization` on `logfire_project`, `logfire_organization_member` and `logfire_organization_members` | v2026-06-25.01 | - |
| `logfire_gateway_provider` | v2026-08-12.01 | logfire-0.13.40 or newer |
| `logfire_gateway_spending_limits`, `logfire_gateway_project_spending_limits` | v2026-10-16.01 | - |
| Delegating project-bound scopes (`project:gateway_proxy`, `project:read_otlp`, `project:write_otlp`) from an organization-wide key | v2026-09-14.01 | logfire-0.13.46 or newer |
| `logfire_schedule`, and `alerts` on `logfire_slo` | v2026-09-23.01 | logfire-0.13.47 or newer |
| `logfire_gateway_provider` with the `azure` vendor | v2026-10-19.01 | - |
| `logfire_gateway_provider` with the `google-vertex` vendor | v2026-10-29.02 | - |
| `logfire_gateway_endpoint` | v2026-10-29.02 | - |
| `logfire_organization_members` | v2026-06-11.01 | - |
| `logfire_organization_member`, which confirms the organization of its key with `GET /api/v1/organization/` | v2026-06-25.01 | - |

The instance version appears in the Logfire UI and in the `Logfire-Version` response header on releases that report a release tag; builds that report only an image identity are treated as unknown. Charts from logfire-0.13.45 report the release tag the chart was built from.

## Required scopes

Each resource states the scopes its calls need. The summary below is the single place to build a provider credential from; the authoritative per-route list is your instance's API documentation at `<base_url>/api/docs` (each operation shows its OAuth scopes).

| Resource | Scopes on the provider credential | Credential shape |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `logfire_project` | `organization:create_project` to create; `project:read` to read; `project:write` to update or delete | An organization-wide key in the project's organization to create; an organization-wide or the project's own key to read and write |
| `logfire_channel` | `organization:read_channel` / `organization:write_channel` | Organization-wide key |
| `logfire_alert`, `logfire_slo` | `project:read_alert` / `project:write_alert` | A key that can reach the project (see below) |
| `logfire_schedule` | `organization:read_channel` / `organization:write_channel` | Organization-wide key |
| `logfire_dashboard` | `project:read_dashboard` / `project:write_dashboard` | A key that can reach the project |
| `logfire_read_token` | `project:read_token` | A key that can reach the project |
| `logfire_write_token` | `project:write_token` | A key that can reach the project |
| `logfire_frontend_application`, `logfire_frontend_application_token` | `project:write_token` / `project:read_token` | A key that can reach the project |
| `logfire_api_key` | `organization:create_api_key` to create; `organization:read_api_key` / `organization:write_api_key` to list, update, or delete; plus every scope the new key is granted | Organization-wide for organization and project scopes; a project-bound scope (`project:read_otlp`, `project:write_otlp`, `project:gateway_proxy`) on an organization-wide key needs `organization:create_api_key` alongside it (delegation only, v2026-09-14.01+) |
| `logfire_gateway_api_key` | `organization:create_api_key`, `organization:read_api_key`, `organization:write_api_key`, and `project:gateway_proxy` | An organization-wide key holding `organization:create_api_key` and `project:gateway_proxy` (delegation only, v2026-09-14.01+), or a project-scoped key for the project |
| `logfire_gateway_provider` | `organization:read` / `organization:write` | Organization-wide key |
| `logfire_gateway_endpoint` | `organization:read_gateway` / `organization:write_gateway` (`organization:read` / `organization:write` are also accepted) | Organization-wide key |
| `logfire_gateway_spending_limits` | `organization:read_gateway` / `organization:write_gateway` (`organization:read` / `organization:write` are also accepted) | Organization-wide key |
| `logfire_gateway_project_spending_limits` | `organization:read_gateway` / `organization:write_gateway` (`organization:read` / `organization:write` are also accepted) | A key that can reach the project |
| `logfire_organization_member` | `organization:read_member` / `organization:write_member`, and `organization:read` unless `organization` is set through an instance-admin key | Organization-wide key |
| `logfire_organization_members` (data source) | `organization:read_member` | Organization-wide key |
| `logfire_organization` | `organization:admin` | A key created in the admin-panel organization (self-hosted only) |
| Any resource, when the provider or `logfire_project` sets `organization` | `instance:admin` (`organization:admin` also works) | A key created in the admin-panel organization (self-hosted only). The scopes of the resource do not apply, because the exchanged token holds a fixed set. See "Managing several organizations on a self-hosted instance". |

Two rules decide what a credential can do:

- **A key can only delegate scopes it holds**, with one exception on Logfire v2026-09-14.01+ — an organization-wide key that also holds `organization:create_api_key` may hold project-bound scopes purely to delegate them. It cannot exercise them itself; it can pass them to the keys it creates.
- **A key reaches a project when it is organization-wide in the project's organization, or project-scoped to that exact project.** A project-scoped key cannot read or manage any other project, even in the same organization.

### Self-hosted bootstrap

The first credential comes from the admin panel; every later one is minted from a key that already holds the scopes it grants:

1. In the admin organization (the one with the admin panel), create an organization-wide key carrying `organization:admin` to manage organizations with `logfire_organization`.
2. In the organization that owns the projects, create an organization-wide key carrying `organization:create_project`, plus `organization:create_api_key` if Terraform will mint other keys. `logfire_project` creates projects in this key's organization.
3. For the AI Gateway, use either credential shape under "Creating gateway keys" below. `logfire_gateway_provider` additionally needs `organization:read` / `organization:write`, and every `logfire_gateway_api_key` sets `project_id`.

### Creating gateway keys

`project:gateway_proxy` is a project-bound scope: a key can only exercise it when it is bound to a project, which is what gives the gateway a project to resolve providers, budgets, and spend against.

On Logfire v2026-09-14.01 or newer, an organization-wide key that also holds `organization:create_api_key` may carry the scope to delegate it. The scope is delegation-only on that key: the key cannot call the gateway itself (it has no project to resolve), but it can create gateway keys for any project in the organization, so one provider credential covers the project, the gateway provider, and the gateway keys:

```terraform
provider "logfire" {
  # An organization-wide key carrying organization:create_api_key,
  # project:gateway_proxy, and the organization scopes used for projects
  # and gateway providers.
  api_key = var.organization_api_key
}

resource "logfire_project" "example" {
  name = "example"
}

resource "logfire_gateway_api_key" "example" {
  name                 = "example-gateway"
  project_id           = logfire_project.example.id
  spending_limit_daily = 25
}
```

If you would rather the credential that manages gateway keys reach only one project, use a project-scoped operator key and a second provider alias for the `logfire_gateway_api_key` resources. Mint it with `organization:create_api_key`, `organization:read_api_key`, `organization:write_api_key`, and `project:gateway_proxy`, from the **organization-level** API keys page with the project selected: the project's own API keys page rejects the organization-level scopes the key also needs, and without `organization:create_api_key` it cannot create anything. On releases before v2026-09-14.01 this is the only shape that works.

```terraform
provider "logfire" {
  # Creates the project with the organization-wide key.
  api_key = var.organization_api_key
}

provider "logfire" {
  alias = "project"
  # A project-scoped key for that project, holding organization:create_api_key
  # and project:gateway_proxy.
  api_key = var.project_scoped_api_key
}

resource "logfire_project" "example" {
  name = "example"
}

resource "logfire_gateway_api_key" "example" {
  provider             = logfire.project
  name                 = "example-gateway"
  project_id           = logfire_project.example.id
  spending_limit_daily = 25
}
```

## Managing several organizations on a self-hosted instance

One instance-admin API key can act in every organization, so you do not need a long-lived key for each one. The provider exchanges the key at `/api/oauth/token` for a token bound to one organization. The token is valid for 15 minutes, or for the maximum token lifetime of the instance when that is shorter. The provider sends that token on organization-scoped requests. `logfire_organization` already works this way. When no organization is set, the provider sends the key itself, as before.

Two arguments choose the organization.

- The provider `organization` argument, or the `LOGFIRE_ORGANIZATION` environment variable, sets the organization for every resource and data source of that provider. The argument wins over the environment variable, and an empty argument turns the environment variable off.
- `logfire_project`, `logfire_organization_member`, and the `logfire_organization_members` data source take their own `organization` argument, which overrides the provider's. One provider block can then manage projects and member roles in many organizations, for example with `for_each`.

The key must hold `instance:admin` (`organization:admin` also works) and must belong to the self-hosted admin organization. Any other key fails with an error that names this requirement.

Requests under `/api/v1/instance/` keep the key itself. These are the organization list and create routes. `logfire_organization` and project import use them, and the provider reads the organization list to check the organization of each resource.

The exchanged token acts as a service, not as a user, so its scopes are its whole permission boundary. Every token holds these scopes:

```text
organization:read organization:write organization:create_project
organization:read_member organization:write_member
organization:read_channel organization:write_channel
organization:create_api_key organization:read_api_key organization:write_api_key
project:read project:write project:read_token project:write_token
project:read_dashboard project:write_dashboard project:read_alert project:write_alert
```

The exchange refuses `instance:*`, `organization:admin`, and project-bound scopes, so the token never holds them. The set leaves out `organization:read_gateway` and `organization:write_gateway`, which instances before 2026-10-15 reject. The gateway routes accept `organization:read` and `organization:write`.

```terraform
provider "logfire" {
  base_url = "https://logfire.example.com"
  api_key  = var.instance_admin_key
}

resource "logfire_organization" "team_a" {
  name = "team-a"
}

resource "logfire_project" "svc" {
  for_each     = toset(["api", "worker"])
  organization = logfire_organization.team_a.name
  name         = each.key
}

provider "logfire" {
  alias        = "team_a"
  base_url     = "https://logfire.example.com"
  api_key      = var.instance_admin_key
  organization = logfire_organization.team_a.name
}

resource "logfire_write_token" "api" {
  provider   = logfire.team_a
  project_id = logfire_project.svc["api"].id
}
```

Terraform cannot use `for_each` on provider blocks. Projects therefore take `organization` as an argument. Resources that take only a project ID, such as tokens, alerts, and dashboards, use a provider alias with `organization` set. Set the alias to `logfire_organization.team_a.name` instead of a literal. The reference makes Terraform destroy the resources of the alias before it destroys the organization. The provider `organization` must be known at plan time, so pass a name and not an attribute that the API computes, such as an ID.

- `logfire_api_key` and `logfire_gateway_api_key` do not work through an organization. The instance refuses to create an API key for a token without a user and answers `403 This credential cannot create API keys`, whatever the scopes. To create API keys in another organization, use a provider alias whose `api_key` belongs to that organization and holds `organization:create_api_key`. `logfire_write_token` and `logfire_read_token` work through the exchange.
- For each resource, the provider records the ID and name of its organization in private state. For a resource reached through the exchange, that is the organization of the exchange. For a resource reached with the key itself, it is the organization that the key reports for itself, which needs `organization:read`. Refresh, update, and destroy check the record against the organization list. A key of the recorded organization proves it by reading its own organization, so it does not need `instance:admin`. If it has neither scope, refresh and destroy fail with `cannot confirm`. Grant `organization:read` or set the provider `organization` with an instance-admin key.
- A resource stays in its organization. If the provider `organization` changes, refresh and destroy fail for the resources of the old organization, and the error names it. Set the provider `organization` back, or remove those resources from state with `terraform state rm` and create or import them in the new organization.
- Setting the provider `organization` does not move existing projects or members. A project or member keeps the organization in its state.
- A resource that has no record, because a key without `organization:read` created it or an older provider did, takes on the organization that the provider names. A provider that moves to another organization then reads or changes the resources of that organization with the same address, such as the single `logfire_gateway_spending_limits` of an organization. Refresh once with the original key and `organization:read` before you change the provider `organization`.
- A project that existed before you set an organization has no record if its key cannot read its own organization. The provider reaches it with the key, and when the key cannot reach it, through the exchange for the organization in its state. The provider then records that organization. If that exchange fails for a reason other than refusing the key, such as a timeout, the refresh fails and the project stays in state.
- A project or member that sets no `organization` stays in the organization of its state. If the provider `organization` then names another organization, the plan fails with `Resource is in another organization`. Set `organization` on the resource to keep it where it is.
- A project or member whose `organization` is not known until apply fails the plan with `Organization not known until apply`. A change would replace the resource, and Terraform cannot check that the new organization exists before it deletes the resource. Apply what produces the organization first with `terraform apply -target`.
- Changing `organization` on a project replaces it. The plan fails if the new organization does not exist, because the replacement would delete the project before the create fails. A member is replaced the same way, which removes the user from the old organization first. The plan does not check that the create can succeed.
- A member is managed only in an organization that the provider can confirm. A key of its own organization needs `organization:read`, so that the provider can name the organization and record it. A key without that scope fails the create and the import before any change, and the error names the scope. An instance-admin key that sets `organization` needs no such scope.
- If an organization is renamed outside Terraform, projects follow it and refresh updates their `organization`. Set `organization` to the new name in the configuration. A provider that still names the old name fails, and the error gives the new name.
- To rename an organization that Terraform manages and that a project or a provider alias uses, apply the rename alone first with `terraform apply -target=logfire_organization.team_a`. Then apply the rest. The projects and the resources of the alias follow the organization without a replacement.
- If an organization is deleted outside Terraform, refresh removes its resources from state, including the resources of a provider alias, and destroy succeeds. A provider alias that takes its `organization` from `logfire_organization` then fails `terraform plan -refresh-only` with `Unknown Logfire organization`, because that mode does not plan the organization again. Run a normal plan or destroy instead.
- `terraform import logfire_project.x team-a/svc` looks in the organization of the key first, then in `team-a` through the exchange.

## Troubleshooting credentials

- `403 This token is missing the required scope: X.` — the provider credential lacks a scope the call needs. Add `X` to the key and retry.
- `403 You do not have permission to access this project` — the credential cannot reach the project it was given. The key is either project-scoped to a different project, created in a different organization than the project, or the project no longer exists in the key's organization. The API answers the same way for all three on purpose, so it cannot be used to probe.
- `403 The ... scope is not granted to the calling credential and cannot be delegated` — the provider credential was asked to mint a key with a scope it does not hold. For `project:read_otlp`, `project:write_otlp`, and `project:gateway_proxy`, an organization-wide key may delegate them only if it also holds `organization:create_api_key` and the instance runs Logfire v2026-09-14.01 or newer; before that, or for a project-scoped operator, see "Creating gateway keys" above.
- `cannot act in organization "x": the api_key must be an instance-admin API key` appears when the provider or a project sets `organization` and the key lacks `instance:admin`. When the provider reads the organization list first, the error says `acting in another organization needs an instance-admin API key` instead. Use a key from the admin organization that holds `instance:admin` or `organization:admin`.
- `token exchange failed (invalid_target): unknown organization` means the organization does not exist. `audience does not match the configured OIDC base URL` means `base_url` differs from the base URL the instance is configured with. The exchange sends `base_url` followed by the organization name as its audience, and the error shows the audience it sent.
- `refusing the redirect to ...` means the instance redirected the token request to another host, or from HTTPS to HTTP. The provider does not send the key there. Set `base_url` to the URL of the instance itself.
- `This resource lives in organization "x", but the provider now acts in "y"` means the provider `organization` changed. See "Managing several organizations on a self-hosted instance".
- `Cannot confirm the organization of this resource` means the resource was recorded through the exchange and the provider now uses a key that can neither read its own organization (`organization:read`) nor list the instance organizations (`instance:admin`). See "Managing several organizations on a self-hosted instance".
- `refusing the redirect to ...` also appears for an API request. The provider sends its key and headers only to the host of `base_url`, and never from HTTPS to HTTP.
- `404 Not Found` from a self-hosted instance — the instance may predate the API being called. See "Self-hosted compatibility" above.

